CONTEXT.md:12:- **Drip staleness cap**: the 3-day bound owned by `DRIP_STALENESS_GRACE_DAYS` / `isDripTooStale` in `OnboardingDripWorker` — a drip whose due date passed by more than the grace window is never sent, permanently. It exists so an outage that pauses drips does not clear into a burst of late "Welcome to day 1!" emails. Sends **at** the boundary (the comparison is strictly `<`, so equality is not stale), skips past it. Enforced at **two points, and both are load-bearing**: the `OnboardingDripScheduler` producer excludes stale pairs from the candidate query, and a **fail-closed chokepoint** in `handleOnboardingDrip` re-checks before any side effect. Producer-only was the original design and was wrong — an operator re-driving a failed row, a future second producer, or a row minted just inside the boundary and stranded through a multi-day worker outage all reach the handler with the producer's check long expired; #679 reached the same conclusion for **Import consent** and for the same reason. The chokepoint runs **before** the `onboarding_drip_log` claim (a refusal must not consume the claim a legitimate re-send would need) and **completes** the row carrying `completion_reason = 'drip_too_stale'` rather than failing it — a policy refusal is the guard working, not an incident, and the marker makes a refusal separable in SQL from a genuine send. Unlike #679's guard it needs no `SELECT … FOR UPDATE`: staleness is a pure function of `subscription_activated_at` and the clock, and nothing mutates the anchor, so there is no race to serialize against. Both enforcement points leave a **durable record** since #738: the handler chokepoint completes its outbox row with `completion_reason = 'drip_too_stale'` (7-day window under ArchivalPoller's completed-row retention), and the scheduler stamps `skipped_at`/`skip_reason` on `onboarding_drip_log` (migration 104) — a table ArchivalPoller never touches, so the scheduler's record is the **permanent** answer to "which drips were dropped as too-late, and when". The stamp doubles as the candidate-query dedup, replacing the old `GRACE + 1` one-day reporting window: a too-late pair — including a backlog already past the window at deploy, which the old window skipped silently — is selected once, recorded (a guarded `ON CONFLICT … DO UPDATE` stamps a dead claim row; a sent or already-skipped row is untouched, and migration 104's CHECK makes skip-and-send unrepresentable), and excluded thereafter. Removing the outer window is re-mint-safe because archival (7 days) strictly exceeds grace (3 days): any pair archival frees re-enters the query already too late, landing in the skip branch, never the queue branch — that arithmetic is load-bearing; do not shrink outbox archival below the grace window. `skip_reason` vocabulary: `DRIP_SKIP_REASONS` in `OnboardingDripWorker.ts`, derived from `OUTBOX_COMPLETION_REASON.DRIP_TOO_STALE` so one grep finds both enforcement points. *(coined in #719; producer-only enforcement corrected at #719 step-7 review A1; observability asymmetry accepted at review A3, then closed by #738/migration 104)*
CONTEXT.md:13:- **Job-failure sink**: `worker/job-failure-sink.ts`, attached to every BullMQ `Worker` alongside `attachEmitterErrorLogger` — on the **terminal** attempt only it raises a Sentry issue (tags `{subsystem, queue, topic}`) rather than the breadcrumb that preceded it. Its terminal test is the **`'failed'`-event** predicate `attemptsMade >= max`, deliberately NOT `isTerminalAttempt` from `src/lib/shared/outbox.ts`: that one is `attemptsMade >= max || attemptsMade + 1 >= max`, written for the **catch block** where `attemptsMade` has not yet been incremented, and using it in the event context fired one attempt early *and* again on the real last one — a duplicate issue plus a false "exhausted its retries" for a job BullMQ then retried successfully. `worker/jobs/judge/failed-hook.ts` keeps its own copy for the same reason and says "do not unify them"; treat any future shared helper here as a trap. It writes **nothing** to `job_outbox`: the **Watchdog** overwrites `last_error` unconditionally when it terminalizes, and a raw driver message can embed contact PII, so durable per-domain error text stays the job of the six domain `'failed'` hooks — Sentry is the durable record of the *handler error* specifically, not of every failure fact. Promoting these from breadcrumbs to issues moved the message onto the one Sentry field `scrubSentryEvent` did not cover, so that scrubber now redacts `exception.values` too. *(coined in #719; terminal predicate and exception scrubbing corrected at #719 step-7 review A2/A6)*
CONTEXT.md:19:- **Per-widget degradation**: the dashboard failure contract — an auxiliary query failure degrades ONLY its widget to an empty-but-valid shape via `widgetOrFallbackFor(source)` and MUST report through `reportCaughtError` (degradation is never silent); only identity anchors (business lookup, aggregate locations list + baseline anchor) stay fatal. *(coined in the #508 reliability guards)*
CONTEXT.md:29:- **Import offer**: the one-time, per-location invitation to run the Review import, created **server-side at the subscribe+connect junction** — activation non-NULL **and** an active `zernio_connections` row, both proven inside `createImportOffer`'s own SQL rather than trusted from callers. Its deadline is `now() + 7 days` **at creation**; there is no junction anchor (owner adjudication 2026-08-06 — anchoring was self-invented, produced two High findings across two review rounds, and was deleted). An offer is **answerable only while it exists and is live**: the guarded transition requires `import_decision = 'pending' AND import_offer_expires_at IS NOT NULL AND > now()`, so migration 100's default `pending` + NULL deadline is deliberately NOT an offer — that `IS NOT NULL` is what stops a direct POST manufacturing consent for a location nothing was ever offered for. Answering is terminal and first-write-wins; a self-healed offer (repaired by the dashboard GET when the junction-time write failed) starts a fresh 7 days at the repair, which is more generous and is the point. Surfaced by `ImportOfferBanner` through `GET/POST /api/dashboard/import-offer`, never from the Redis-cached dashboard payload. **Inheritance on re-add copies a decision, and a deadline only when that deadline is already in the past** (#677 D12): re-adding a location whose archived row `declined` or let the offer expire does not re-offer the import — the past deadline is copied so the new row still derives expired — but a **live** deadline is left NULL, so the new row earns a fresh offer at its OWN junction. Copying a live deadline would present an answerable offer for a location with no active connection, bypassing the very junction proof `createImportOffer` exists to enforce. Distinct from **Import consent**, which is the stored decision record this offer collects. _Avoid_: import prompt, backfill offer, "the banner" (that is the surface, not the offer). *(coined in #677; spec `docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-05-consented-review-import-design.md` §3, §5.3)*
CONTEXT.md:32:- **Reviews list**: the owner's single inventory of every review for a location — the surface the consented Review import lands on. It is **not a new page**: `dashboard/reviews/history` (the former Past Replies archive) is widened in place to include never-replied reviews and becomes it (#671, 2026-08-08; spec §6.3a S1). Grouped `Unanswered` then `Answered` per D6, newest-first inside each group, `PAGE_SIZE` 50. Distinct from the **Needs Action** queue at `dashboard/reviews`, which survives unchanged and answers a different question: that surface filters to `pending_approval` + live auto-post veto windows off the Redis-cached dashboard payload (`ReviewFeedCard.tsx:59-66`, `LIMIT 10`) and can never show an imported review. A review with a pending draft appears in **both** — in the list as a `Draft ready` cross-link with no `Generate` button. Per-row actions split by risk: `Generate` (never-replied) expands the row inline (`InlineGeneratePanel`), `Regenerate` (already-replied) opens the `EditReplyDrawer` two-pane comparison because publishing there **replaces** the owner's live Google reply (spec §6.4). As built (#727), every affordance derives from two orthogonal axes modelled server-side (ADR 0018; replaces #678's six-valued `row_state` precedence chain `recover → regenerate → held → draft_ready → generating → generate`): the **Posted-reply axis** (`reply_state`: `none` / `posted` / `orphan`) — what exists on Google for the review; owns the Unanswered/Answered grouping and the reply panel — and the server-internal **Replacement-draft axis** (`draft_state`: `none` / `generating` / `ready` / `held`, intra-axis precedence held > ready > generating > none), which **never crosses the wire**. The card receives only `reply_state` plus the **Action slot** (`row_action`: `generate` / `generating` / `draft_ready` / `regenerate` / `recover` / `none`) — the single affordance derived from the full 3×4 cross-product (`deriveAxes` + `deriveRowAction`, `src/lib/past-replies/replies-view.ts`). A held draft never projects an affordance of its own (invariant #4 as structure): `none`×held and `posted`×held project `row_action 'none'` — a posted row keeps its live Google reply visible and silently withdraws Regenerate — while `orphan`×held keeps `recover`, because recovery addresses the orphan incident, not the draft. The `held` value itself never reaches the browser: the card reconstructs the "Reply on hold" panel for an unanswered held row from `reply_state 'none'` + `row_action 'none'`. The `gap` / `historical` section split derived from `subscription_activated_at` is deleted here, surviving only as a per-row `ProvenanceBadge` (`posted_before_joining` / `replied_on_google` / `auto_approved` / `owner_approved`); `PAST_REPLIES_VIEW_ENABLED` was **retired with it at #678** — the list renders unconditionally — while `EDIT_REPLIES_ENABLED` is kept (it gates the live-Google mutation) and `editRepliesEnabled()` (`src/lib/past-replies/flags.ts`) is now its only reader. _Avoid_: Past Replies, the archive, History tab (all name the pre-#671 surface), All Reviews page (it is a tab, not a page). *(coined in #671; spec §6.3a, §6.4; implemented at #678; remodelled at #727 per #723)*
CONTEXT.md:34:- **Review-time anchor**: `LEAST(review_date, detected_at)` — the timestamp the reputation KPIs (review volume MoM, 8-week sentiment trend, avg star) bucket on, so a consented Review import lands in its true months/weeks instead of spiking the import month (and instantly yields a real historical trend). LEAST ignores NULLs: a NULL `review_date` falls back to `detected_at`; a clock-skewed future `review_date` clamps to `detected_at` (unclamped it vanishes from the trend's `generate_series` LEFT JOIN). Workflow surfaces — response rate and the MonthlyImpactWorker digest — deliberately stay on `detected_at`: an import is account activity, and imported unanswered reviews are meant to drag the rate (#675 nudge reasoning). Time-to-respond carries the companion validity filter `approved_at >= detected_at`, so a pre-existing Google reply imported by reconcile can never contribute a negative duration. One owner: `reviewTimeAnchor` (`src/lib/kpi/fragments.ts`), composed by the rendered dashboard payload — velocity, rating breakdown, avg star and sentiment trend per-location, and velocity + rating breakdown in the All-Locations aggregate (#757). _Avoid_: review_date anchor (names the column, not the clamp), import exclusion (rejected design — imported rows participate, in their true buckets). *(coined at #688 grilling 2026-08-10; ADR 0019)*
CONTEXT.md:42:- **Backlog depth**: the count of `status='pending'` rows in a dispatch-queue table (`job_outbox`, `analytics_events`) — **total** pending, including not-yet-due retry rows (NOT "claimable only"; no `available_at <= NOW()` filter). A saturation signal against the Capacity target. Single owner: `queryBacklogDepth(sql)` (`src/lib/shared/backlog-depth.ts`), read by both the `/internal/queue-health` endpoint and the HealthAlertPoller. *(coined in the #472 observability effort, ticket #480)*
CONTEXT.md:43:- **Backlog depth alert threshold**: the per-table pending-row count (`HEALTH_ALERT_BACKLOG_DEPTH_THRESHOLD`, default **1500**, sized to the #474 Capacity target) above which HealthAlertPoller emits a `warning` (`backlog_depth:<table>`) — a saturation nudge, not dropped work; the 5s OutboxPoller drain normally clears it. *(coined in the #472 observability effort, ticket #480)*
CONTEXT.md:45:- **Capacity-burst drill**: the two-arm operational drill (ticket #505/#480) driven by the checked-in `scripts/burst-drill-load.ts` against shared staging, proving outbox DISPATCH-drain of a burst of pending `job_outbox` rows plus Backlog depth observability — **not** full ingest→draft→notification (LLM) pipeline throughput. **Headroom arm**: ~1000 immediately-claimable rows paced over 10 minutes; drain rate (~600/min) outpaces injection, backlog stays low, drains ≤30 min, and `backlog_depth:job_outbox` does **not** fire — proves drain + no false alarm. **Over-threshold arm**: ~1600 future-dated rows that count toward Backlog depth but the poller cannot yet claim, so pending holds above the Backlog depth alert threshold across a poller tick and `backlog_depth:job_outbox` fires; a scoped `release` then clears the injected rows and the alert clears (pending drops below threshold **and** a subsequent HealthAlertPoller cycle completes a clean check-in emitting no such finding — `emit.ts` has no separate recovery event). *(coined in the #505 differential + burst-drill effort)*
CONTEXT.md:67:- **Body redaction**: the 90-day post-send NULLing of `rendered_subject`/`rendered_body` **and emptying of `interpolation_inputs` to `'{}'`**, with `body_redacted_at` stamped and the metadata row kept forever — data minimization on third-party PII held on the contact's behalf, timed comfortably past the 14-day attribution window and past bounce/complaint feedback lag. The source goes with the prose: `interpolation_inputs` carries `contact_first_name` and `visit_note`, so redacting only the rendered text would delete the sentence and keep its ingredients. This is enforced in the schema, not merely in the sweep — `solicitation_outreach_body_presence` requires `body_redacted_at IS NULL OR interpolation_inputs = '{}'::jsonb`, so no future writer can stamp a redaction and skip the erasure. It costs the post-90-day reproducibility of the `unverifiable_fact` provenance verdict (#623 §2.4), which was already lost at the same instant anyway: that verdict is computed over `subject + body`, both of which this same sweep deletes. It is **not a delete**, which is why the retention inventory carries it as a novel shape (redact-in-place, plus purge-by-cascade). **Redaction implies TERMINAL, and that is one window on two clocks**: a `sent` row ages from `sent_at`, while a row that settled without ever sending (`withheld`/`cancelled`/`failed`/`unknown`) has no `sent_at` and ages from `rendered_at`. A LIVE row (`rendered`/`queued`/`sending`) is never redactable at any age — a bodyless live attempt can neither send nor settle, and wedges its batch behind `solicitation_outreach_one_live_per_ask`, which is the hazard the rule exists for. #624 shipped the rule as "implies **sent**", which made the stamp unrepresentable on the four never-sent terminals and left their bodies and inputs retained forever (#636 / step-7 S2); 092 widened it to the hazard's actual shape. Swept by two bounded arms of the existing `ArchivalPoller` — no new poller, because the single-replica P2 duplicate-processing budget was already spent — each oldest-first with `SKIP LOCKED`, capped at 5000 rows per cycle and logged as saturated at its own cap. A body is never logged. _Avoid_: body purge, body expiry. *(coined in #619 D6b/D6c; swept in #624; completed in #636)*
CONTEXT.md:69:- **Attested postal address**: the owner-attested `businesses.solicitation_postal_address` plus its attestation quartet (`_attested_at`, `_attested_by_user_id`, `_attestation_version`, `_attested_digest`) — the CAN-SPAM §7704(a)(5) sender address baked **verbatim** into every ask footer, and a send precondition. Deliberately **not** `google_place_address`, which is Google's `formattedAddress`: it can be a service-area value like `"Austin, TX, USA"`, is not owner-attested, cannot express a USPS-registered PO box, and is NULLed by the offboarding purge (spec §7.5). Address and attestation are **all-or-nothing by DB CHECK**, and the attestation is welded to the address's **current bytes** by a second CHECK over `_attested_digest` — so no code path can read an unattested address as attested, and no writer can change the stored bytes without recomputing that digest. That guarantee is **narrower than "the attestation is always current"**, and the narrow statement is the load-bearing one: the digest CHECK proves the digest matches the CURRENT address, not that the preserved `_attested_at` / `_attested_by_user_id` / `_attestation_version` ever covered those bytes — a writer that changes the address AND recomputes the digest while keeping the old metadata still passes. Closing that residual needs `OLD` vs `NEW`, which a CHECK cannot see; the trigger was declined by owner decision (2026-08-04, plan "Design decisions" #1). The accidental path — a future `UPDATE … SET solicitation_postal_address = $1` that forgets the attestation — is closed; the residual is forgery rather than oversight. `_attested_by_user_id` is a **plain UUID with no FK** — a point-in-time capture, per migration 093's written lesson that 086's `ON DELETE CASCADE` meant deleting a staff member deleted their uploads. Because there is no FK, the *writer* verifies the actor. _Avoid_: business address, mailing address, footer address. *(coined in #656 — migration 099; owned by `src/lib/solicitation/postal-address.ts`)*
CONTEXT.md:74:- **Restraint policy**: the deterministic, sentiment-blind rule set that decides send vs withhold for each Solicitation candidate — the refusal logic is the product; everything else displays its decisions. No LLM in the decision path. *(coined in the §9 solicitation roadmap; locked in ticket #533)*
CONTEXT.md:75:- **Withhold reason**: one code from the closed six-reason enum (`no_consent`, `suppressed`, `already_reviewed`, `stale_visit`, `over_contacted`, `velocity_banked`) explaining why a candidate was not asked. Every reason is sentiment-blind by construction; `recent_negative` is permanently rejected (ADR 0012). First-match precedence; one reason per withhold. _Avoid_: refusal code, skip reason. *(locked in ticket #533)*
CONTEXT.md:76:- **Freshness window**: the per-vertical day count beyond which a candidate's last visit is too stale for a genuine review ask (`stale_visit`). v1 is a hard window; learned decay curves are the future upgrade behind the same gate. *(locked in ticket #533)*
CONTEXT.md:91:- **Contact (Solicitation)**: the deduped identity of one person at one location, keyed `(business_id, normalized handle)`. An incoming CSV row matches a contact if EITHER normalized handle (email or phone) matches; a matched contact merges in a handle it lacked; a row whose handles match two different contacts is rejected `ambiguous_identity` — never auto-merge. *(locked in ticket #535 grilling)*
CONTEXT.md:92:- **Visit**: one append-only ledger row per contact per calendar date, unique on `(contact_id, visit_date)`, carrying the optional staff/job note and an FK to its upload. Visit dedupe makes overlapping re-uploads fully idempotent (`duplicate_visit` is informational). This is the visits-ledger tracer of design doc §9 decision 3. *(locked in ticket #535 grilling)*
CONTEXT.md:93:- **Normalized handle**: the canonical contact identifier — lowercased/trimmed email or E.164 phone (US assumed for 10-digit) — computed once at CSV ingest and stored on the contact. It is the future suppression-ledger join key (per channel), never the contact row id: delete + re-upload must not un-suppress an opted-out person. *(coined in ticket #535 grilling)*
CONTEXT.md:94:- **Consent attestation**: the per-upload record — attesting user id, timestamp, versioned attestation-text constant — required as a blocking step at CSV upload; contact/visit rows inherit its provenance via their upload FK. It is NOT a per-row consent claim — per-handle consent state is the **Consent ledger**'s job (below, #625). *(coined in ticket #535 grilling; per-row semantics resolved by #625)*
CONTEXT.md:95:- **Consent ledger**: the `solicitation_consent` table (migration 093, #625) — one current-state row per `(channel, normalized_handle, business_id)` answering "which attestation covers this handle, for this channel, at this location." Written ONLY by csv ingest inside the per-business advisory-locked transaction (D8), and only for the resolved contact's RETAINED email (§7.1 deletability — `consentEligibleEmail`); phone handles mint zero rows (DR3, email-only). **Monotone forward** (§4.2): the guarded upsert (`WHERE EXCLUDED.attested_at > attested_at`) upgrades to a newer attestation and never downgrades, so replayed jobs are no-ops and an in-flight pre-deploy v1 job cannot overwrite a v2 row; a version change emits `solicitation_consent_upgraded`, a same-version re-attestation bumps silently. Send-eligibility is `attestation_version ∈ SEND_ELIGIBLE_ATTESTATION_VERSIONS` (v2-only — v1's text never named email sending, which is the `consent_attested` tautology #625 killed); an ineligible-version row withholds `no_consent` **case (b)** (upload_id + attestation_version populated), no row at all is **case (a)** (both null — the whole pre-#625 legacy cohort, no backfill by decision; #629's case-(a) copy must be self-sufficient). Revocation lives in the suppression ledger, never here (D7 — no `revoked_at`, ever): consent fails closed, suppression fails open (DR1), so consent rows hard-delete with their contact (by handle, same tx) and via an explicit purge-stage sweep (spec §7.2 as corrected — businesses are tombstoned, the cascade never fires on purge). RLS deny-all; no TTL (§7.3). _Avoid_: consent flag, attested boolean, consent snapshot (that is what #630 stamps on outreach at dispatch). *(coined in #620 grilling; shipped by #625, PR #640)*
CONTEXT.md:110:- **Attestation never overrides suppression**: stated verbatim because it is the most likely way self-serve re-subscribe gets built by accident — an owner re-uploads a CSV, re-attests consent, and someone reasons "they attested, so we're covered." **Attestation is the owner asserting a relationship; suppression is the recipient revoking one. The second outranks the first, permanently.** Consent and suppression are independent inputs to `evaluateAsk`; the withhold-reason precedence (`no_consent` before `suppressed`) governs only **which reason is reported when both apply** and is not an override relationship — it will be misread as one unless this is written down. The mechanism backing the rule is #535 §6's handle keying, which is why an opt-out cannot be laundered by delete-and-re-upload. Its build-time expression is **populate, do not pre-filter** (§4.2): the batch build stamps `candidate.suppression` and `evaluateAsk` owns the withhold, so the shipped precedence-2 `suppressed` gate never becomes dead code. _Avoid_: consent overrides, re-consent, re-permission. *(coined in #621 grilling; D6 §8.1)*
CONTEXT.md:114:- **Voice Report Card**: the owner-facing section on `/settings/voice` reporting what voice learning inferred — five Register traits, each a plain-language claim backed by one before→after snippet from the owner's own edits. Derives on read from EXACTLY the corpus the prompt path uses (`getVoiceCorpusStatus`/`getApprovedResponsePairs`); below activation (5 posted owner-edited replies) it shows "N of 5" progress and never partial traits. Deterministic only — no LLM, nothing persisted, no flag/tier gate. *(coined in #536 prototype; locked in #540 grilling)*
