     1	# RepWorth Domain Glossary (seed)
     2	
     3	- **Review source**: a provider adapter (Zernio today) that ingests reviews and normalizes them to the `NormalizedReview` DTO; no source-specific fields leak past it. *(draft — to be sharpened in grilling sessions)*
     4	- **Response / draft**: the LLM-generated reply to a review; it is only a draft until an approval method is recorded. *(draft — to be sharpened in grilling sessions)*
     5	- **Approval method**: how a response earned the right to be posted — `owner` (explicit owner action) or `auto_policy` (server-only gated runtime, disabled by default). *(draft — to be sharpened in grilling sessions)*
     6	- **Moderation hold**: a `high`-severity moderation flag writes a `flagged_responses` row (status `open`) and suppresses owner notification. A draft is **held** while its flag is `open`; the single read-side spelling of that predicate is `openFlagExistsSql` (`src/lib/moderation/held-draft.ts`, #730 — its header also inventories the deliberately distinct consumers that do NOT consolidate onto it). Any disposition off `open` (admin `approved_for_owner_review`, `dismissed_false_positive`, `requires_regeneration`) closes held-ness; only `approved_for_owner_review` additionally re-notifies the owner. *(draft — sharpened at #730 review; was: "only admin approval releases it", which conflated flag-release with re-notification)*
     7	- **Account**: the billing parent (subscription, quotas, stored tier prices). **Business**: one Google Business Profile location under an account. *(draft — to be sharpened in grilling sessions)*
     8	- **Outbox job**: follow-up work recorded in `job_outbox` in the same transaction as the state change; OutboxPoller dispatches it to BullMQ. *(draft — to be sharpened in grilling sessions)*
     9	- **Full access**: subscription statuses (`active`, `trialing`) that `subscriptionGrantsFullAccess` treats as entitled; workers gate on it, never on a raw status literal. *(draft — to be sharpened in grilling sessions)*
    10	- **Poller cadence**: a background loop's tick interval — owned by the code default (`DEFAULT_INTERVAL_MS`), overridable per-env via its `*_INTERVAL_MS` env var. Changing cost posture means changing the code default (rides PR + staging); the env var is a per-env operational override, never the authoritative value. *(coined in the cost-optimization wayfinder effort)*
    11	- **Watchdog**: the recovery queries inside a dispatch poller (OutboxPoller's recover/terminate/stale-dispatching UPDATEs, AnalyticsDispatchPoller's stale-reclaim) that reclaim rows orphaned by crashes — distinct from dispatch itself, and allowed to run on a slower cadence than dispatch. Its terminate arm writes `last_error = 'Stale dispatched entry exceeded max_attempts'` (`OutboxPoller.ts:140-143`) **unconditionally**, overwriting whatever was there: that text describes the **reclaim**, never the handler failure underneath it. Reading it as a diagnosis is what let #719 hide for three months — the diagnosis lives in Sentry, via the **Job-failure sink**. *(coined in the cost-optimization wayfinder effort; sharpened in #719)*
    12	- **Drip staleness cap**: the 3-day bound owned by `DRIP_STALENESS_GRACE_DAYS` / `isDripTooStale` in `OnboardingDripWorker` — a drip whose due date passed by more than the grace window is never sent, permanently. It exists so an outage that pauses drips does not clear into a burst of late "Welcome to day 1!" emails. Sends **at** the boundary (the comparison is strictly `<`, so equality is not stale), skips past it. Enforced at **two points, and both are load-bearing**: the `OnboardingDripScheduler` producer excludes stale pairs from the candidate query, and a **fail-closed chokepoint** in `handleOnboardingDrip` re-checks before any side effect. Producer-only was the original design and was wrong — an operator re-driving a failed row, a future second producer, or a row minted just inside the boundary and stranded through a multi-day worker outage all reach the handler with the producer's check long expired; #679 reached the same conclusion for **Import consent** and for the same reason. The chokepoint runs **before** the `onboarding_drip_log` claim (a refusal must not consume the claim a legitimate re-send would need) and **completes** the row carrying `completion_reason = 'drip_too_stale'` rather than failing it — a policy refusal is the guard working, not an incident, and the marker makes a refusal separable in SQL from a genuine send. Unlike #679's guard it needs no `SELECT … FOR UPDATE`: staleness is a pure function of `subscription_activated_at` and the clock, and nothing mutates the anchor, so there is no race to serialize against. Both enforcement points leave a **durable record** since #738: the handler chokepoint completes its outbox row with `completion_reason = 'drip_too_stale'` (7-day window under ArchivalPoller's completed-row retention), and the scheduler stamps `skipped_at`/`skip_reason` on `onboarding_drip_log` (migration 104) — a table ArchivalPoller never touches, so the scheduler's record is the **permanent** answer to "which drips were dropped as too-late, and when". The stamp doubles as the candidate-query dedup, replacing the old `GRACE + 1` one-day reporting window: a too-late pair — including a backlog already past the window at deploy, which the old window skipped silently — is selected once, recorded (a guarded `ON CONFLICT … DO UPDATE` stamps a dead claim row; a sent or already-skipped row is untouched, and migration 104's CHECK makes skip-and-send unrepresentable), and excluded thereafter. Removing the outer window is re-mint-safe because archival (7 days) strictly exceeds grace (3 days): any pair archival frees re-enters the query already too late, landing in the skip branch, never the queue branch — that arithmetic is load-bearing; do not shrink outbox archival below the grace window. `skip_reason` vocabulary: `DRIP_SKIP_REASONS` in `OnboardingDripWorker.ts`, derived from `OUTBOX_COMPLETION_REASON.DRIP_TOO_STALE` so one grep finds both enforcement points. *(coined in #719; producer-only enforcement corrected at #719 step-7 review A1; observability asymmetry accepted at review A3, then closed by #738/migration 104)*
    13	- **Job-failure sink**: `worker/job-failure-sink.ts`, attached to every BullMQ `Worker` alongside `attachEmitterErrorLogger` — on the **terminal** attempt only it raises a Sentry issue (tags `{subsystem, queue, topic}`) rather than the breadcrumb that preceded it. Its terminal test is the **`'failed'`-event** predicate `attemptsMade >= max`, deliberately NOT `isTerminalAttempt` from `src/lib/shared/outbox.ts`: that one is `attemptsMade >= max || attemptsMade + 1 >= max`, written for the **catch block** where `attemptsMade` has not yet been incremented, and using it in the event context fired one attempt early *and* again on the real last one — a duplicate issue plus a false "exhausted its retries" for a job BullMQ then retried successfully. `worker/jobs/judge/failed-hook.ts` keeps its own copy for the same reason and says "do not unify them"; treat any future shared helper here as a trap. It writes **nothing** to `job_outbox`: the **Watchdog** overwrites `last_error` unconditionally when it terminalizes, and a raw driver message can embed contact PII, so durable per-domain error text stays the job of the six domain `'failed'` hooks — Sentry is the durable record of the *handler error* specifically, not of every failure fact. Promoting these from breadcrumbs to issues moved the message onto the one Sentry field `scrubSentryEvent` did not cover, so that scrubber now redacts `exception.values` too. *(coined in #719; terminal predicate and exception scrubbing corrected at #719 step-7 review A2/A6)*
    14	- **Connection wedge** (the "wedge"): a postgres.js pool connection stalled server-side in `state=active, wait_event=ClientRead` — a socket-level stall waiting for client bytes that never arrive. No server-side timeout heals it, and `withTimeout` does not cancel the underlying work, so the leaked slot never returns to the pool; one wedge per cycle → full-pool starvation. *(coined in the #500 pool-wedge self-heal)*
