    28	- **Import consent**: the per-location decision record for the Review import offer — `import_decision` (`pending` | `accepted` | `declined`), `import_decision_at`, `import_offer_expires_at`, columns on `businesses` (migration 100, #673). **Expiry is derived, never stored** (#670): "expired" = `pending AND import_offer_expires_at <= now()`, computed on read by one predicate helper — no poller, no `expired` enum value, and nothing fires at the expiry moment. The single owner of the predicate and of every write is `src/lib/reviews/import-consent.ts` (#677): `importConsentStateSql` composes the derivation into a larger query (**callers must alias `businesses` as `b`**), `readImportConsent` returns the row, `createImportOffer` stamps the deadline at the junction, and `recordImportDecisionAndEnqueue` owns the acceptance transaction. Enforcement today is **at the four product enqueue paths** (#677) — connect finalize, the Stripe activation resume, reactivation catch-up, and the owner's decision route — each gating on `accepted` while holding the `businesses` row lock. The **fail-closed worker guard shipped at #679** (PR #698): `guardImportConsent` (`worker/jobs/zernio/job-eligibility.ts`) runs in `ZernioBackfillWorker` before `guardZernioJobEligibility` and before the run-row INSERT, and proceeds only on `accepted` — so declined, derived-expired, pending and a missing `businesses` row, including hand-inserted rows i.e. the admin path, all no-op-complete with a logged `reason`. Since #695 a refused row is also **marked for the archival window** — `job_outbox.completion_reason = 'import_consent_blocked'` (the `OUTBOX_COMPLETION_REASON` vocabulary, column from migration 083) — so a policy refusal is separable by SQL from a genuine completed import, which before was only inferable from the log line plus the absence of a `zernio_backfill_runs` row. **It is an operational-window signal, NOT an audit record**: `ArchivalPoller` deletes completed `job_outbox` rows 7 days after `completed_at`, and a refusal completes rather than fails, so it takes the 7-day arm — the same life `owner_unreachable` has always had (ADR 0010), a property of the sink rather than of #695. What outlives the window is `import_decision` / `import_decision_at` on `businesses`, plus the log line under the log sink's own retention. ONE marker for all four refusal states: it records THAT a refusal happened, and the state that caused it lives only in the log line — a later read of `businesses` can disagree (a `pending` refusal the owner then accepts) or find nothing (the `missing` case). Nothing reads it in code: no poller, no alert, no admin surface — a refusal is the guard working. No backfill, so a NULL on a row predating #695 is not evidence of a genuine import. The `job_outbox`-boundary alternative was weighed and declined at #695 (ADR 0016, `docs/adr/0016-import-consent-enforcement-locus.md`). D8's "admin path included" now holds at the one chokepoint every enqueue path shares. It is deliberately a SEPARATE function, not a `jobKind` branch: a merged guard would have two callers, one of which must never reach consent code, so splitting makes live-ingest independence structural rather than a runtime branch. Its whole body runs inside `sql.begin` with `SELECT … FOR UPDATE` + `markOutboxCompletedWithReason(tx, …)`, and both halves are load-bearing — without them the guard can read `pending` while the owner accepts, the acceptance's in-flight dedup inserts no replacement, and the guard completes the row: `accepted` with no completed run and no runnable outbox row, i.e. silent permanent loss for a customer who said yes. Accepted-but-never-completed stays retryable via the `completed_at IS NULL` sentinel (spec §5.1). Distinct from the solicitation **Consent attestation** / **Consent ledger** below — different domain, different tables, no shared machinery. _Avoid_: bare "consent" (ambiguous with solicitation), backfill consent. *(coined in #670; spec §5.3; worker-guard