    89	## Solicitation contacts & visits (locked 2026-07-23 — ticket #535)
    90	
    91	- **Contact (Solicitation)**: the deduped identity of one person at one location, keyed `(business_id, normalized handle)`. An incoming CSV row matches a contact if EITHER normalized handle (email or phone) matches; a matched contact merges in a handle it lacked; a row whose handles match two different contacts is rejected `ambiguous_identity` — never auto-merge. *(locked in ticket #535 grilling)*
    92	- **Visit**: one append-only ledger row per contact per calendar date, unique on `(contact_id, visit_date)`, carrying the optional staff/job note and an FK to its upload. Visit dedupe makes overlapping re-uploads fully idempotent (`duplicate_visit` is informational). This is the visits-ledger tracer of design doc §9 decision 3. *(locked in ticket #535 grilling)*
    93	- **Normalized handle**: the canonical contact identifier — lowercased/trimmed email or E.164 phone (US assumed for 10-digit) — computed once at CSV ingest and stored on the contact. It is the future suppression-ledger join key (per channel), never the contact row id: delete + re-upload must not un-suppress an opted-out person. *(coined in ticket #535 grilling)*
    94	- **Consent attestation**: the per-upload record — attesting user id, timestamp, versioned attestation-text constant — required as a blocking step at CSV upload; contact/visit rows inherit its provenance via their upload FK. It is NOT a per-row consent claim — per-handle consent state is the **Consent ledger**'s job (below, #625). *(coined in ticket #535 grilling; per-row semantics resolved by #625)*
    95	- **Consent ledger**: the `solicitation_consent` table (migration 093, #625) — one current-state row per `(channel, normalized_handle, business_id)` answering "which attestation covers this handle, for this channel, at this location." Written ONLY by csv ingest inside the per-business advisory-locked transaction (D8), and only for the resolved contact's RETAINED email (§7.1 deletability — `consentEligibleEmail`); phone handles mint zero rows (DR3, email-only). **Monotone forward** (§4.2): the guarded upsert (`WHERE EXCLUDED.attested_at > attested_at`) upgrades to a newer attestation and never downgrades, so replayed jobs are no-ops and an in-flight pre-deploy v1 job cannot overwrite a v2 row; a version change emits `solicitation_consent_upgraded`, a same-version re-attestation bumps silently. Send-eligibility is `attestation_version ∈ SEND_ELIGIBLE_ATTESTATION_VERSIONS` (v2-only — v1's text never named email sending, which is the `consent_attested` tautology #625 killed); an ineligible-version row withholds `no_consent` **case (b)** (upload_id + attestation_version populated), no row at all is **case (a)** (both null — the whole pre-#625 legacy cohort, no backfill by decision; #629's case-(a) copy must be self-sufficient). Revocation lives in the suppression ledger, never here (D7 — no `revoked_at`, ever): consent fails closed, suppression fails open (DR1), so consent rows hard-delete with their contact (by handle, same tx) and via an explicit purge-stage sweep (spec §7.2 as corrected — businesses are tombstoned, the cascade never fires on purge). RLS deny-all; no TTL (§7.3). _Avoid_: consent flag, attested boolean, consent snapshot (that is what #630 stamps on outreach at dispatch). *(coined in #620 grilling; shipped by #625, PR #640)*
