    97	## Solicitation suppression & unsubscribe — locked 2026-07-28 (ticket #621)
    98	
    99	Extends the contacts & visits entries above; it never replaces them. The **Consent ledger**
   100	records what the owner asserted, this records what the recipient revoked — and the second
   101	outranks the first, permanently.
   102	
   103	- **Suppression ledger**: the `solicitation_suppressions` table (migration 096, #643) — one row per opt-out, keyed on the **Normalized handle** per channel and never on the contact row id, so delete-and-re-upload cannot launder it (#535 §6). Rows are **first-write-wins** (`ON CONFLICT … DO NOTHING` against two partial unique indexes, each predicate re-spelled byte-for-byte): a `complaint` arriving after an `unsubscribe_link` leaves the earlier row alone, because the state is already correct and the history belongs in the provider webhook records #622 §5 already stores. Seven closed sources (§3.1) — `unsubscribe_one_click`, `unsubscribe_link`, `bounce_permanent`, `complaint`, `provider_suppressed`, `admin_manual`, `owner_manual` — exported as `as const` arrays by the single authoritative `src/lib/solicitation/suppression.ts`, never string literals at call sites. Soft bounces, `delivery_delayed`, `email.failed` and any ambiguous bounce classification **never** suppress (§3.2); `provider_suppressed` is the known-dirty source, because Resend's list is team-wide across every domain and API key including transactional (§3.3). **No expiry, ever** — a TTL on an opt-out is a violation with a cron job attached. Both evaluation points are **existence checks**, never a comparison against `suppressed_at`, which deliberately sidesteps the postgres.js microsecond-truncation class. The handle is stored **raw, not hashed** (§6.4). RLS deny-all — server-owned in the `auto_approval_policies` posture (invariant #14), because a direct client read would expose the `account_id` provenance §7.4 forbids surfacing. Two derived rules bound it: **DR1** — `SOLICITATION_SEND_ENABLED` masters *sending* only, so flipping it off leaves every ledger write, the ingress and the landing page fully live. **DR2** — the ledger is **solicitation-only**; a transactional or notification path that consults it stops delivering approval emails to an owner who opted out as somebody else's customer, reaching invariant #3 sideways. _Avoid_: blocklist, do-not-mail flag, opt-out table, suppression list. *(coined in #621 grilling; schema + domain core by #643, PR #647)*
   104	- **Suppression scope**: the `scope ∈ {'global','account'}` discriminator recording **who authored the opt-out**, not how widely it happens to apply. `global` is the recipient's — or the mail ecosystem's on their behalf — keyed `(channel, normalized_handle)` across every tenant, legitimate because the recipient spoke and defensible under one shared sender (`reviews@feedback.repworth.net`), where a complaint scores against the domain no matter which business sent. `account` is the owner's `owner_manual` do-not-contact, keyed `(channel, normalized_handle, account_id)`; global would let Owner A silently degrade Owner B's reach with no recipient having said anything — a cross-tenant write dressed as a product feature. The source/scope pairing is **enforced by a DB CHECK** (G11), because `owner_manual + global` would otherwise insert cleanly; it is **one-directional** — `scope='account'` requires `account_id NOT NULL`, while a `global` row MAY still carry `account_id` as provenance. **Lookup contract**: a handle is suppressed for an account iff a `global` row exists for `(channel, handle)` OR an `account` row exists for `(channel, handle, account_id)` — owned by the one exported fragment `suppressionMatchSql` and never re-implemented at a call site (G7: no `isSuppressed()` helper, because the second consumer does not exist yet). **Global wins** when both match (G3): reporting the account row would let an owner clear it under §7.6, watch the contact stay suppressed, and have no idea why. Retention splits on the same axis (D7) — global rows are `allowed_limited_retention` and survive an account purge with `account_id` intact, account rows hard-delete. _Avoid_: per-account suppression, tenant scope, suppression level. *(coined in #621 grilling; D1/D5)*
   105	- **Unsubscribe token**: a row in `solicitation_unsubscribe_tokens` (migration 096, #643) — a SHA-256 `token_hash` via `node:crypto` (invariant #10) plus the redemption target, deliberately a **dedicated table rather than a column on `solicitation_asks`** (D8) so redemption still works after the sender is gone. Raw tokens exist only in memory and in the sent message, never in Postgres, outbox payloads or logs (invariant #5). **No expiry**: CAN-SPAM §7704(a)(3) requires links operate ≥30 days, and there is no expiry value better than none. `account_id`/`business_id`/`ask_id` are provenance only and are **scrubbed to NULL** by the offboarding purge while `token_hash`, `normalized_handle`, `channel` and `sent_at` are kept — the row outlives the account that minted it. `outreach_id` is `ON DELETE SET NULL` (G1, amending B9): CASCADE would delete retained tokens through the `solicitation_outreach` → `solicitation_batches` → `contact_csv_uploads` chain and break §5.2, while no FK at all would leave a scrubbed row carrying a stale pointer nothing could detect. Provenance obeys a **one-way implication** (G12) — `account_id IS NULL` implies `business_id` and `ask_id` are null — deliberately excluding `outreach_id`, which SET NULL may null independently while the account is still live. A second redemption is a no-op success, and unknown and used tokens are indistinguishable to the caller (D3). _Avoid_: unsubscribe link row, opt-out token, magic link. *(coined in #621 grilling; D8; table by #643, PR #647; redemption by #644)*
   106	- **Unsubscribe redemption**: exchanging an Unsubscribe token for a `scope='global'` Suppression ledger row — the only thing #644 does. **Claim first, then suppress**: a single `UPDATE … SET redeemed_at = NOW() WHERE token_hash = $1 AND redeemed_at IS NULL RETURNING account_id, normalized_handle, channel` is the one write-ordering primitive, and `recordSuppression` is fed from the provenance that statement locked, never from a value read earlier. **`business_id` is deliberately NOT returned** — `recordSuppression` has no field for it, so claiming it would be provenance the ledger cannot record, and G12's one-way implication (`account_id IS NULL` ⇒ `business_id IS NULL`) already makes it redundant for the null case. Ordering it the other way round lets a replayed spent link silently undo an audited **Correction path** removal, and lets a concurrent purge write a suppression carrying stale non-null provenance against §5.2. Four closed outcomes, and the third is the one that is easy to lose: `redeemed` · `already_suppressed` · `already_redeemed_not_suppressed` · `unknown_token`. **A spent token is not proof of a live suppression** — `redeemed_at IS NOT NULL` proves only that it was *once* redeemed, so the spent branch reads the ledger explicitly; with no live global row the outcome is `already_redeemed_not_suppressed`, which mints no receipt and deliberately does **not** re-suppress. This is checked against a global-only existence query rather than `suppressionMatchSql`, which answers a different question (may we contact this handle *for this account*) and would be satisfied by an `account`-scoped row that makes the success sentence false. **Honest failure**: a token that does not resolve returns `unknown_token` rather than a success-shaped no-op, because reporting success on a #630 mint race is precisely the lost opt-out this ticket exists to prevent. _Avoid_: consume the token, burn the link, mark unsubscribed. *(coined in #621 grilling; ordering locked across review rounds 2–4 of #644)*
   107	- **Success receipt**: the server-minted HMAC that gates whether the `/u/<token>` success copy may be **shown** — `<issuedAtMs>.<base64url HMAC-SHA256>` over the token hash, keyed on `SESSION_COOKIE_SECRET`, 10-minute TTL, verified with `timingSafeEqual`. Minted only after a write commits, and only for `redeemed` or `already_suppressed`. It exists because a bare `?done=1` rendered "You're unsubscribed" with no token resolved and no suppression written. **Necessary but never sufficient**: verification proves a suppression existed when the receipt was *minted*, and the Correction path can remove it during the TTL — so the page renders success only when the receipt verifies **and** a live global row exists **at render time**. *A page may claim "you are unsubscribed" only from a ledger read taken during that render.* The receipt authorises nothing and grants no access; it is enumeration-resistance evidence, not an access token. _Avoid_: confirmation token, success flag, done parameter. *(coined in #644 owner-run adversarial reviews 1 and 3 — three false-success vectors, one root cause: asserting a fact about mutable ledger state from evidence decoupled in time from it)*
   108	- **Neutral page**: the single identical `/u/<token>` render for unknown, malformed, and already-redeemed tokens — §5.4 enumeration resistance made structural, so no caller can distinguish the three. It carries the `SOLICITATION_UNSUBSCRIBE_MAILTO` fallback so a recipient never dead-ends, and omits that line entirely when the address is unset rather than rendering a broken one. Distinct from the **button page**, which names the originating business, and from the success view, which names **none** — the §5.5-versus-§5.4 conflict resolved in §5.4's favour, because rendering §5.5's named-business sentence on the success page would require resolving a now-redeemed token and make it distinguishable from an unknown one on that URL. _Avoid_: error page, not-found page, generic page. *(§5.5/§5.4 conflict resolved at #644 plan time with owner sign-off)*
   109	- **Correction path**: the **admin-only, audited removal** of a suppression row that is factually wrong — an admin typo on a manual entry, an ambiguous bounce the #622 §5.3 fail-safe did not catch, or a `provider_suppressed` mirror that originated on the transactional side (§3.3). Framed as **error correction, never re-subscribe**: not exposed to owners, not to recipients, no self-serve entry point, and it writes an audit record naming who removed the row and why. It exists because "no re-subscribe" without it is itself a defect — a typo would be permanently unfixable and the answer to "please put me back" would be "we cannot, ever," a worse story than the one the rule was avoiding. **The sink is a `solicitation_suppression_corrections` row (migration 098), not `account_audit_log`** (P-1): it takes `writeAccountAuditLog`'s *posture* — the record lands with the act it describes — but deliberately not its table, because `account_audit_log.account_id` is `NOT NULL REFERENCES accounts(id) ON DELETE CASCADE` and the rows §8.2 exists to correct carry **no account** (an `admin_manual` global row entered from a forwarded mailbox reply names none; neither does a `provider_suppressed` mirror originating on the transactional side), while a CASCADE would additionally delete the compliance record along with the account it merely names as provenance. Atomicity is **structural, not conventional**: `correctSuppression` is ONE data-modifying CTE whose `DELETE … RETURNING` feeds the INSERT (P-9), so the delete cannot commit without its record even if a call site forgets a transaction, and a row already gone returns `not_found` rather than an orphan record. The table carries **no FKs at all** (P-2) — a compliance record must outlive every referent it names, and the row it describes is deleted by the same statement that writes it — but it **does** snapshot the corrected row's `normalized_handle`, `account_id`, `created_by_user_id` and `note` (P-3, D-A11). Those four are the evidence behind the assertion being corrected: `suppression_id` points at a deleted row, so without the handle the record cannot identify its subject, and without the creator and the §7.3 originating message reference it cannot show the original entry was legitimate. `account_id` is not merely provenance — for a `scope='account'` row it **is** the Suppression scope key (§1.2, non-null by construction), so omitting it left account-scoped corrections unable to say whose do-not-contact was removed. Carrying that evidence makes the table account-linked, so it is **classified** — `retain_compliance_limited_pii`, the disposition its siblings `solicitation_suppressions` and `solicitation_unsubscribe_tokens` already carry (D7) — rather than renamed to evade classification: `original_*`-style column names slip past `accountLinkedTablesFromMigrations()` on a word-boundary technicality, and naming data to dodge a governance check is worse than the gap it hides. Distinct from **§7.6 owner clearing**, where an owner removes an `account`-scoped row they created themselves because no recipient ever spoke; that act writes to **`account_audit_log`** instead (P-4), since it is account-scoped with a non-null `account_id` and is emphatically not the compliance act — different act, different sink, deliberately not interchangeable. An owner may **never** clear a `global` row, and the UI must not present the two controls as the same thing. _Avoid_: unsuppress, re-subscribe, opt back in, restore contact. *(coined in #621 grilling; D6 §8.2; shipped by #645 — migration 098, `src/lib/admin/suppressions-service.ts`, the AAL2-gated `/admin/suppressions` console under **Safety**. The P-n citations above are #645's plan decision register, `docs/superpowers/plans/2026-08-03-645-manual-suppression-paths.md`)*
   110	- **Attestation never overrides suppression**: stated verbatim because it is the most likely way self-serve re-subscribe gets built by accident — an owner re-uploads a CSV, re-attests consent, and someone reasons "they attested, so we're covered." **Attestation is the owner asserting a relationship; suppression is the recipient revoking one. The second outranks the first, permanently.** Consent and suppression are independent inputs to `evaluateAsk`; the withhold-reason precedence (`no_consent` before `suppressed`) governs only **which reason is reported when both apply** and is not an override relationship — it will be misread as one unless this is written down. The mechanism backing the rule is #535 §6's handle keying, which is why an opt-out cannot be laundered by delete-and-re-upload. Its build-time expression is **populate, do not pre-filter** (§4.2): the batch build stamps `candidate.suppression` and `evaluateAsk` owns the withhold, so the shipped precedence-2 `suppressed` gate never becomes dead code. _Avoid_: consent overrides, re-consent, re-permission. *(coined in #621 grilling; D6 §8.1)*
   111	
   112	## Voice Report Card (locked 2026-07-24 — ticket #540, prototype #536)
   113	
   114	- **Voice Report Card**: the owner-facing section on `/settings/voice` reporting what voice learning inferred — five Register traits, each a plain-language claim backed by one before→after snippet from the owner's own edits. Derives on read from EXACTLY the corpus the prompt path uses (`getVoiceCorpusStatus`/`getApprovedResponsePairs`); below activation (5 posted owner-edited replies) it shows "N of 5" progress and never partial traits. Deterministic only — no LLM, nothing persisted, no flag/tier gate. *(coined in #536 prototype; locked in #540 grilling)*
   115	- **Register trait**: one of five deterministic voice patterns (greeting, length, sign-off, point of view, critical-review register) derived heuristically from `VoiceExample` pairs. Renders only when its pattern holds in a strict majority of applicable pairs with ≥2 conforming; Critical reviews is exempted (applicable = negative pairs per `classifySentiment`, detects at ≥1, claim scoped to its evidence when single). Undetected traits are omitted — the card never claims what wasn't learned. *(coined in #536 prototype; detection bar locked in #540 grilling)*
   116	
   117	## Content-freshness gate (locked 2026-08-09 — ADR 0017, tickets #724/#728)
   118	
   119	- **Content-freshness gate** — the lane-independent check at the persist boundary of response generation: under the review `FOR UPDATE` lock `persistResponseAndNotify` takes as its first statement, the current `review_content_hash` is re-read and compared with the hash the worker loaded before the LLM call. Both non-null and different → **skip-the-INSERT**: the job completes (never fails) with the one-owner completion reason **`stale_review_content`** (`OUTBOX_COMPLETION_REASON.STALE_REVIEW_CONTENT`), writing NOTHING — the `(review_id, version)` slot stays free for the colliding fresh job. A null hash on either side passes: it is a freshness check, not a provenance proof. The auto lane's stricter hash gate in `evaluateAutoApprovalCandidate` is unchanged defense in depth (invariant #3).
     1	# RepWorth — Review Deduplication & Ingestion
     2	
     3	## Core Principle
     4	
     5	Identity is stable (immutable fields only). Content is mutable (detected via
     6	hash comparison). These are separate concerns — never mix them.
     7	
     8	> **Multi-location naming note (since migration 034):** `reviews.business_id`
     9	> and `responses.business_id` now mean "location_id" — one Google Business
    10	> Profile location, not the billing parent. The dedup logic below is
    11	> unchanged: a review's identity is scoped to its `business_id` (= location).
    12	> Two locations under the same `account_id` never collide because each
    13	> Place ID has its own `business_id`. When working on aggregate dashboards
    14	> or CSV bulk-imports, scope by `account_id` first, then fan out per
    15	> `business_id`.
    16	
    17	> **Auto-approval lane note (since the auto-approval feature, migration 055):** the
    18	> new-vs-content-changed distinction this dedup logic produces now feeds `origin_lane`
    19	> on the `generate_response` job. A **first-time new** review → `live_ingest_new` (the
    20	> ONLY lane eligible for auto-approval); a content-changed / supersede-regenerate review
    21	> → `live_ingest_update`, and Zernio backfill/import → `backfill` — both always manual.
    22	> So if you change what counts as "new" vs "update/import" here, you are also changing
    23	> what can auto-post to Google. See the auto-approval design spec §5 (origin lanes).
    24	> (Since #675 the consented Review import enqueues **no generation at all**, so the
    25	> `backfill` lane gains no new rows — the vocabulary survives for pre-#675 rows.)
    26	
    27	> **Trial note (since migration 069 / PR #362):** the new `'trialing'` subscription
    28	> status is treated like `'active'` for ingestion — `trialing` accounts ARE included in
    29	> the backfill-resume enqueue (`worker/ingress/stripe/shared.ts`) and the per-cadence
    30	> review pollers. `accounts.subscription_activated_at` (mirrored to
    31	> `businesses.subscription_activated_at`) is set at **trial start** for trials (not at
    32	> first payment); it still gates the backfill-resume enqueue and is recorded on the
    33	> run row, but since #675 it **buckets nothing** — the gap/historical split is dead
    34	> vocabulary; the import selects purely by the Import window (see the Consented
    35	> Review import section below).
    36	> Don't re-gate ingestion on "paying" status; gate on `subscriptionGrantsFullAccess`.
    37	
    38	## Stable Identity Construction (SerpAPI mode)
    39	
    40	Identity must NOT include mutable text (review body, star rating can be
    41	edited by the reviewer on Google).
    42	
    43	**Priority order:**
    44	1. Use SerpAPI's raw review ID if present (`provider_review_id`)
    45	2. Fall back to SHA-256 hash of immutable fields:
    46	   `SHA-256(reviewerName + "|" + reviewDate + "|" + reviewerProfileUrl)`
    47	
    48	**Why prefer source-provided IDs:** Synthetic hashes collide on common/
    49	anonymous reviewer names (e.g. "A Google User" + same date from different
    50	locations). Always use the source-provided identifier when available.
    51	
    52	**Fields used:**
    53	```typescript
    54	provider = 'serpapi_poll'
    55	provider_review_id = serpapi_raw_id ?? SHA256(name + "|" + date + "|" + profileUrl)
    56	```
    57	
    58	## Synthetic Resource Names
    59	
    60	These are constructed consistently so they're stable across re-ingestion:
    61	
    62	```
    63	provider_review_name  = 'serpapi://{placeId}/reviews/{provider_review_id}'
    64	provider_location_name = 'serpapi://{placeId}'
    65	```
    66	
    67	## Mutable Content Detection
    68	
    69	```typescript
    70	review_content_hash = SHA256(starRating + "|" + reviewText)
    71	```
    72	
    73	On every ingestion, compare `review_content_hash` to the stored value.
    74	If different: the review was edited — treat as an update, NOT a new review.
    75	
    76	### Sentiment is (re)classified at the same write sites (Wave 3 PR 5a)
    77	
    78	`reviews.sentiment_*` is populated by `classifySentiment(starRating)`
    79	(`src/lib/reviews/sentiment.ts`, deterministic star-based `deterministic_star_v1`)
    80	at exactly the content-write points dedup decides:
    81	
    82	- **NEW** review → the INSERT in `process-review.ts` sets sentiment.
    83	- **UPDATED** (content hash changed) → the content UPDATE recomputes sentiment.
    84	- **`insertImportedReview`** (`worker/jobs/zernio/zernio-ingest-shared.ts`, the
    85	  external-reply path that bypasses `processReview`) also sets it.
    86	- **UNCHANGED** and the status-only writes (deleted→new revival, soft-delete) do
    87	  **not** touch sentiment.
    88	
    89	So any change that counts as a content update for dedup also refreshes sentiment;
    90	adding a new review-content write path means adding the `classifySentiment` call
    91	there too (migration 052's SQL backfill uses the same star→label/score mapping).
    92	
    93	## Collision Handling
    94	
    95	If two reviews produce the same identity key but have different content
    96	on first encounter (before any `review_content_hash` is stored):
    97	
    98	- Do NOT silently merge
    99	- Insert into `review_exceptions` with `type = 'identity_collision'`
   100	- Log the conflicting provider_review_ids for ops review
   101	
   102	## Zernio Mode
   103	
   104	Zernio provides canonical review IDs. Use them directly — no SHA-256 fallback
   105	needed.
   106	
   107	> **Google-native resource name capture (migration 070):** `ZernioReview.name`
   108	> carries Google's review resource name
   109	> (`accounts/{a}/locations/{l}/reviews/{r}`). Ingest persists it to
   110	> `reviews.google_review_name` via `normalizeGoogleReviewName`
   111	> (`src/lib/reviews/zernio-normalize.ts` — fail-open to NULL; capture is
   112	> provenance, never a reason to fail ingest) at the review-content write sites:
   113	> `processReview` INSERT, the content-changed UPDATE (COALESCE — never clobbers
   114	> a stored value), and `insertImportedReview` (INSERT + conflict-arm backfill).
   115	> It is **NOT dedup identity** — that remains `(provider,
   116	> provider_review_name)`. Consumer: the future direct-GBP posting adapter,
   117	> which addresses replies by exactly this resource name. Outbox dedup index (`job_outbox_ingest_zernio_review_inflight`)
   118	handles duplicate webhook deliveries:
   119	
   120	```sql
   121	-- migration 030 partial unique index
   122	(aggregate_id, (payload->>'zernio_review_id'))
   123	WHERE topic = 'ingest_zernio_review' AND status NOT IN ('completed', 'failed')
   124	```
   125	
   126	Webhook fan-out uses `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING` against this index.
   127	
   128	**Per-location routing (multi-location hardening, 2026-06):** the webhook fan-out is
   129	scoped per location. `worker/ingress/zernio/zernio.ts` reads
   130	`parsed.locationIdFromPayload` (parsed from the review resource name
   131	`accounts/{a}/locations/{loc}/reviews/{r}` by `parseZernioReviewWebhook`) and
   132	narrows the fan-out to `zernio_connections WHERE zernio_account_id = …
   133	AND zernio_location_id = <loc>` — so a review only lands on the location it
   134	belongs to (no cross-routing across an account's locations). The ingest worker
   135	then **fails closed** on a location mismatch: `extractLocationFromReviewName`
   136	(`src/lib/reviews/zernio-normalize.ts`) returning null →
   137	`ZernioIngestUnparseableLocationError`; a parsed location ≠ the connection's
   138	`zernio_location_id` → no-op (it's a sibling location's review). The fan-out also
   139	**skips archived locations** (PR A spend-leak fix): `guardZernioJobEligibility`
   140	short-circuits jobs for an archived `business`, so an archived location never
   141	ingests or generates a draft.
   142	
   143	## Consented Review import (#675, PR #689)
   144	
   145	`ZernioBackfillWorker` (topic `zernio_initial_backfill`) is the consented
   146	Review import executor — NOT a backfill that answers anything. Selection is
   147	the pure `selectImportWindow`: newest **100** reviews with `createTime ∈
   148	[now − 12 months, live-ingest cutoff)`; the CONTEXT.md glossary owns both the
   149	**Import window** and **Live-ingest cutoff** terms. The cutoff
   150	(`liveIngestCutoff` in `zernio-ingest-shared.ts`, shared with
   151	`ZernioRecentReviewPoller`) makes lane ownership disjoint by construction:
   152	live lane ≥ cutoff, import < cutoff. Null `connected_at` → poller owns
   153	nothing, import unbounded above.
   154	
   155	- **No generation, no notifications**: unanswered reviews store via
   156	  `importReview` (draft-free since #674 — zero `job_outbox` writes); answered
   157	  reviews go through `reconcileExternallyAnsweredReview({ consentedImport:
   158	  true })` regardless of `EXTERNAL_REPLY_IMPORT_ENABLED`.
   159	- **Counters**: runs write collapsed `imported_count`/`skipped_count` (+
   160	  `oldest_skipped_create_time`) on `zernio_backfill_runs`. The four legacy
   161	  `gap_*`/`historical_*` columns are **GONE** — migration 103 (#680) dropped
   162	  them from both Supabase projects 2026-08-08, after they had frozen at DEFAULT
   163	  0 since #675. There are exactly three counter columns now; naming a legacy one
   164	  raises 42703. `skipped_count` counts cap overflow + PHI/malformed only —
   165	  out-of-window reviews are unselected, not "skipped".
   166	- **Pagination**: `ZERNIO_IMPORT_MAX_PAGES` (default 6) caps `listReviews`
   167	  paging (pageSize 50, page-boundary dedup by review id). Early stops
   168	  (target met / floor passed) are trusted only while ordering is clean; a
   169	  non-descending page disables both for the run and it pages to feed end,
   170	  best-effort (the staging sandbox feed IS non-descending in practice — see
   171	  lab-notes). Cap exhausted with `nextPageToken` set and <100 in-window →
   172	  **terminal** `ZernioImportPaginationCapExhaustedError` (failed outbox row +
   173	  Sentry; NO `review_exceptions` row — distinct from the ingest worker's
   174	  `zernio_pagination_cap_exhausted` exception path); `completed_at` stays
   175	  NULL so ops can bump the cap and re-enqueue (consent permitting, spec §5.3).
   176	- **Idempotency**: one-shot per business for `run_reason='initial'`
   177	  (`completed_at` sentinel); reactivation runs key on `correlation_id`.
   178	- **Consent**: the enqueue gates shipped at **#677** (below, which absorbed
   179	  #676) and the fail-closed worker pre-flight at **#679**. The release
   180	  constraint both carried is **discharged**.
   181	
   182	### Who may enqueue an import (#677, PR #691)
   183	
   184	Enforcement today is at the **four product enqueue paths**, each gating on
   185	`import_decision='accepted'` while holding the `businesses` row lock. All four
   186	delegate to ONE `enqueueInitialImport` in `src/lib/reviews/import-consent.ts`:
   187	
   188	| Producer | File |
   189	|---|---|
   190	| Connect finalize | `src/app/api/zernio/connect/_shared/finalize/finalize-tx.ts` |
   191	| Stripe activation resume | `worker/ingress/stripe/shared.ts` |
   192	| Reactivation catch-up | `src/lib/locations/archive-location-tx.ts` |
   193	| The owner's decision route | `src/app/api/dashboard/import-offer/route.ts` |
   194	
   195	Before #677 these were three hand-copied guards that had **already diverged**,
   196	and none matched the banner's read model — a completed *reactivation* run
   197	permanently suppressed the one-time initial import while `ImportOfferBanner`,
   198	correctly scoped to `initial`, reported `importing` forever on a 4s poll. The
   199	shared function scopes its guard to `br.run_reason='initial' AND completed_at
   200	IS NOT NULL`, matching `readImportOfferView` exactly.
   201	
   202	Reactivation is deliberately **not** routed through it: it enqueues with
   203	`backfill_reason: 'reactivation'`, and the worker's `ON CONFLICT … WHERE
   204	run_reason = 'initial'` does not dedup it, because the archived-period catch-up
   205	must recur.
   206	
   207	### The chokepoint (#679, PR #698; block reason + ADR 0016 at #695, PR #714)
   208	
   209	The four gates above are producer-side, so they only bind code that goes through
   210	a producer. `guardImportConsent` (`worker/jobs/zernio/job-eligibility.ts`) closes
   320	| `provider_review_id` | SerpAPI ID or SHA-256 hash | Canonical Google review resource name |
   321	| `provider_review_name` | `serpapi://{placeId}/reviews/{id}` | Google resource name |
   322	| `provider_location_name` | `serpapi://{placeId}` | Google location resource name |
   323	| `review_content_hash` | SHA-256(starRating + "\|" + text) | Same — unchanged |
   324	
   325	Steps for migration:
   326	1. Build `gbp-api-source.ts` implementing `ReviewSource` interface
   327	2. Normalize to the same `NormalizedReview` DTO
   328	3. Swap `provider` value on new ingestion
   329	4. Migrate existing `provider='serpapi_poll'` rows to `provider='google'`
   330	   with canonical Google resource names
   331	5. Content hash logic stays identical — no re-hashing needed
   332	
   333	The outbox pattern, approval flow, response generation, and notification
   334	pipeline are completely unaffected. This is a mechanical swap at the
   335	ingestion boundary only.
   336	
   337	## Reconciliation (Externally-Answered Reviews)
   338	
   339	When Zernio indicates `reviewReply !== null` on a fetched review, call
   340	`reconcileExternallyAnsweredReview` instead of `processReview`. This marks
   341	the review as already-responded externally, avoiding generating a duplicate
   342	AI response for something the owner already handled manually.
   343	
   344	
   345	### External-reply ingestion path (feat/past-replies-view, 2026-05-02)
   346	
   347	`reconcileExternallyAnsweredReview` was extended on `feat/past-replies-view`
   348	with two NEW flag-gated branches that turn the silent
   349	"previously-unseen" / "matched but no active responses" no-ops into actual
   350	ingestion. Gated by `EXTERNAL_REPLY_IMPORT_ENABLED='true'` — when off,
   351	today's silent-no-op behavior is preserved verbatim.
   352	
   353	**Branch 1 — "previously-unseen review with reply" (no match):** INSERTs a
   354	new `reviews` row (with `external_reply_ingested_at = NOW()` to mark its
   355	provenance) AND a new `responses` row with:
   356	- `status = 'posted'` — the reply already exists on Google.
   357	- All three text columns = `canonicalizeReplyText(reviewReply.comment)`.
   358	- `edited_text = NULL` — voice learning won't pick it up
   359	  (`src/lib/responses/voice-learning.ts:30` filters `edited_text IS NOT NULL`).
   360	- `llm_provider`/`llm_model`/`prompt_tokens`/`completion_tokens` all NULL
   361	  — signals "not RepWorth-generated".
   362	- `version = 1` (no prior responses for this brand-new review row).
   363	- `approved_at = posted_at = reviewReply.updateTime`.
   364	
   365	**Branch 2 — "matched but no active responses":** INSERTs the response row
   366	only (the review row already exists). Same shape as Branch 1, but
   367	`version = COALESCE((SELECT MAX(version) FROM responses WHERE review_id =
   368	${reviewId}), 0) + 1` because terminal rows (failed, superseded) might
   369	already occupy version 1. Migration 031's `responses_one_posted_per_review`
   370	partial unique index protects against duplicate `posted` rows on retry.
   371	
   372	**Branch 3 — "matched WITH active responses":** the supersede/protect logic
   373	is flag-independent — protecting our own already-posted replies from being
   374	clobbered by a webhook echo remains the same regardless of the flag. Since
   375	#675 the branch has a **consented tail**: in the consented lane
   376	(`consentedImport: true`), after superseding stale active drafts the external
   377	reply IS stored (PHI-gated via `phiGateAndImportExternalReply`) — UNLESS
   378	`protectedIds.size > 0`, i.e. one of the active responses is our own posted
   379	reply, which already represents the text locally (an imported `posted` row
   380	would duplicate it). The live lane (flag path) still converges on a later
   381	poll instead; a one-shot import cannot, which is why the tail exists.
   382	
   383	**Race-safety on the review INSERT:** Branch 1 uses
   384	`ON CONFLICT (provider, provider_review_name) DO UPDATE SET id = reviews.id RETURNING id`.
   385	NOT `DO NOTHING + RETURNING` — under concurrent transactions,
   386	`DO NOTHING + RETURNING` returns no row when the conflicting INSERT loses
   387	the race AND the same statement's snapshot can't see the just-committed
   388	sibling. `DO UPDATE` always RETURNs the row id, whether INSERTed or
   389	pre-existing. The no-op `SET id = id` triggers a row-level lock + xmin
   390	bump but no observable data change.
   391	
   392	**Idempotency target on the response INSERT:**
   393	`ON CONFLICT (review_id) WHERE status = 'posted' DO NOTHING RETURNING 1`
   394	— uses migration 031's partial unique index. The `RETURNING 1` lets
   395	`insertImportedResponse` return a boolean indicating whether the INSERT
   396	actually fired vs hit the conflict. The caller uses that to gate the
   397	counter bump (`bumpExternalReplyIngestedCount`) so retries / repeated
   398	backfills don't inflate `external_reply_ingested_count`.
   399	
   400	**Loose-equality `reviewReply` guards:** The `listReviews` Zernio client
   401	method now normalizes `reviewReply` to either `null` or an object (never
   402	`undefined`) at the API chokepoint via `?? null`. Downstream callers
   403	(`ZernioBackfillWorker`, `ZernioReviewIngestWorker`,
   404	`reconcileExternallyAnsweredReview`) also use loose-equality `!= null` /
   405	`== null` as defense-in-depth — if any future code path constructs a
   406	`ZernioReview` outside `listReviews`, the omitted-field case still doesn't
   407	misroute as "answered" and crash on `.comment`.
   408	
   409	**The published-reply text** is shown on the **Reviews list**
   410	(`dashboard/reviews/history`) via the `PastReplyCard` component. That surface
   411	is **no longer flag-gated**: `PAST_REPLIES_VIEW_ENABLED` was retired at #678
   412	(spec `docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-05-consented-review-import-design.md`
   413	§6.3a S4) because the Reviews list is the consented **Review import**'s only
   414	destination — keeping the gate would have let `import_decision='accepted'`
   415	coexist with the imported reviews landing on a `notFound()`. Consequence for
   416	rollout: `EXTERNAL_REPLY_IMPORT_ENABLED` alone now decides both the write and
   417	the exposure, so there is no silent-soak window — flipping it on shifts the
   418	dashboard "total responded" count and surfaces the imported replies in the
   419	same step. `EDIT_REPLIES_ENABLED` still separately gates every mutation
   420	(Regenerate → republish), which is the risk that actually warranted a flag.
   421	
   422	## Onboarding-demo lane (PR #2, 2026-05-04)
   423	
   424	The pre-pay demo (`/onboarding/start` → `/onboarding/demo`) ingests reviews
   425	DIFFERENTLY from the post-pay Zernio path. Key separations to keep in mind
   426	when touching either codebase:
   427	
   428	**No `reviews` table writes.** The demo SerpAPI fetch goes through
   429	`fetchSerpApiReviews` and the result is snapshotted as JSONB into
   430	`onboarding_demos.reviews_snapshot`. Nothing is INSERTed into `reviews`.

