    81	## Addendum (2026-07-16) — capacity target locked; SPOF + LLM-cap deferral sharpened
    82	
    83	Resolved in the scalability-hardening wayfinder map
    84	([map #472](https://github.com/RepWorth/ai-review-responder/issues/472),
    85	tickets [#474](https://github.com/RepWorth/ai-review-responder/issues/474) and
    86	[#475](https://github.com/RepWorth/ai-review-responder/issues/475)). This
    87	refines the posture above — it does not reverse it.
    88	
    89	**Capacity target (#474):** 50 businesses at launch → 250 locations at 6
    90	months (~100–150 accounts); ~125 reviews/day fleet-wide steady state; design
    91	burst 1000 ingest events in 10 minutes, drained within 30 minutes with a
    92	queue-health alert at ~1500 pending outbox rows; peak 10 concurrent dashboard
    93	renders. Single-replica worker posture stays.
    94	
    95	**Global LLM concurrency cap (#475): deferral held, trigger sharpened.**
    96	While the worker is single-replica, the de facto global LLM cap is the
    97	*summed* BullMQ concurrency across the LLM-calling queues (draft generation,
    98	weekly summary, monthly impact, review triage, judge, onboarding demo). That
    99	sum stays **≤8**, documented where `workerOpts` are defined. The global Redis
   100	semaphore (beside the per-business one in `src/lib/costs/concurrency-guard.ts`)
   210	## Vercel-side connection-multiplication drill (executed 2026-07-20 — PASS)
   211	
   212	The central-hazard gap flagged in the post-drill note below was closed by a
   213	Vercel-side load drill on the same staging stack (Supabase ref
   214	`kubdiqdhqbhwmqfditeg`, feature deployed). This is the deferred measurement that
   215	the worker-pool drill did not exercise.
   216	
   217	**Method.** A bounded ramp of concurrent authenticated `GET /api/dashboard/kpis`
   218	renders (the heavier of the two `sqlRead` routes — a multi-query per-location KPI
   219	wave) against `https://staging.repworth.net`. One ephemeral active owner was
   220	seeded (magic-link session minted through the app's own `/api/auth/callback`
   221	token_hash lane; the Vercel SSO wall was passed with a Protection-Bypass-for-
   222	Automation header, not by disabling protection) and deleted after the run — no
   223	residual rows. Load driven from a one-off concurrent-fetch harness; backend
   224	sessions sampled every 2 s from a **direct** (5432) connection so the sampler did
   225	not perturb the Supavisor pool. Phases: 10 concurrent × 20 s → 20 × 20 s →
   226	40 × 45 s.
   227	
   228	**Vercel request health — PASS.** 1,575 requests (≈ 1.6 K invocations incl.
   229	warm-up), **0 errors, 0 timeouts, 100 % HTTP 200** across all phases. At sustained
   230	40-concurrent: rps ≈ 22, KPI latency p50 1.6 s / p95 3.2 s / **p99 3.5 s** — under
   231	the 8 s `sqlRead` `statement_timeout`. Vercel Observability (all-environments,
   232	Fluid, region iad1) corroborated: Error 0 % / Timeout 0 %, Active CPU p75 191 ms,
   233	Memory p95 308 MB, TTFB p75 497 ms.
   234	
   235	**The three central-hazard bounds — all PASS:**
   236	- **Backend sessions (vs 57 usable):** baseline 8 → **peak 41 total** (40 net of
   237	  the sampler's own connection) during the 40-concurrent phase = **≈ 70 % of 57**;
   238	  peak `active` 12; **`idle in transaction` = 0 throughout** (no #505 wedge —
   239	  fan-out ≤ pool held). After load stopped the count settled to a ~34 → 31
   240	  Supavisor **warm-backend-pool** plateau that reaps over minutes (pooler keeps
   241	  backends warm for reuse; not a leak — all `idle`).
   242	- **Warm Vercel isolates (vs ≤ 16):** not a first-class Vercel metric, so
   243	  **derived ≈ 4–6** from the ~32 Vercel-attributable backend sessions
   244	  (peak 40 − ~8 baseline) at ~6–8 pooled connections per isolate — and
   245	  **corroborated directly by Start Type = Hot 98.5 % / Cold 1.1 % / Prewarmed
   246	  0.4 %** under the burst: Fluid served all 1.6 K requests by **reusing a small
   247	  warm set** rather than fanning out to many isolates. Far under 16.
   248	- **Total client connections (vs 200 Max-Clients):** at this scale Supavisor held
   249	  ≈ 1 backend per pooled client connection (they show `idle`/`ClientRead`), so the
   250	  client-side count ≈ the backend count — **peak ≈ 41 system-wide, Vercel share
   251	  ≈ 32**, i.e. **~16–20 % of 200**. The ≤16-isolate × 11 arithmetic (≈ 198) is not
   252	  reached because Fluid packing keeps the isolate count to ~4–6, not 16.
   253	
   254	**Net.** Driving ~4× current peak dashboard traffic (40 concurrent vs ~10 today)
   255	produced ~4–6 isolates, ~41 peak connections (70 % of the 57 backend ceiling,
   256	~20 % of the 200 client ceiling), and zero connection-exhaustion failures. The
   257	central hazard is now **measurement-proven**, not merely budget-bounded.
   258	
   259	**Residual (honest) — the compound worst case is untested.** This Vercel drill ran
   260	with a **quiet worker** (baseline 8); the earlier worker burst-drill ran with
   261	Vercel quiet (peaked ~19). Neither exercised **both bursting simultaneously**. The
   262	compound worst case (worker #474 burst ~19 + a 40-concurrent Vercel burst ~32, less
   263	the ~8 shared baseline) ≈ **~52 / 57 (~91 %)** — headroom exists but is the tightest
   264	scenario, and is precisely what the pool-wait metric surfaces and what the reversal
   265	trigger below governs (raise the Supabase compute tier before raising `DB_POOL_MAX`
   266	or `READ_POOL_MAX_CEILING`). At current traffic this compound scenario is far off.
   267	
   268	## Post-drill note: what the WORKER-POOL staging drill did and did NOT prove (central-hazard scope)

